# Cloudflare deploy

Two Workers ship from this repo: the **web** app (`apps/web`, Next.js via OpenNext) and the **cron** Worker (`apps/cron`, Hono). Both bind the same D1 database (`DB`) and R2 bucket (`STORAGE`).

## One-time provisioning

### D1

Both Workers bind the same database (`apps/web` and `apps/cron`).

{{> cloudflare/d1-provisioning}}

### R2

Create the bucket (and `{{projectName}}-storage-preview` if you use `wrangler dev` preview on the web app), declining Wrangler's offer to add the binding itself, as for D1:

```bash
{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{installedBinaryRunner}} wrangler r2 bucket create {{projectName}}-storage --no-update-config{{/inDirectory}}
```

An account that never used R2 refuses it with error `10042` (`Please enable R2 through the Cloudflare Dashboard`). Enable R2 at `https://dash.cloudflare.com/<account id>/r2/overview` (the account id is the one `wrangler whoami` prints, see Production URL), then run the command again. Create the bucket before the first deploy: the web Worker binds `STORAGE`, and a deploy with no bucket behind it fails with error `10085`.
{{> cloudflare/production-url}}

### Secrets

The web app lists `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` and `BETTER_AUTH_URL` (the production URL of the web app, learned above) under `secrets.required` in `apps/web/wrangler.jsonc`, so a deploy fails until both are set; the cron Worker has none. The first deploy of a new Worker takes them from a file, written in Build & deploy. Once the Worker exists, change one with `{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{installedBinaryRunner}} wrangler secret put BETTER_AUTH_SECRET{{/inDirectory}}` and the same for `BETTER_AUTH_URL`.

## Build & deploy

Run these from the repository root, in this order, the first time. The production URL is the one learned above.

**1. Give the build the production URL.** Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` is given to the build, as a shell variable (it wins over `.env`). Next.js freezes it into the client bundle and the server code, and the server-side tRPC client calls the app at it, so a build with the local value deploys a Worker that calls `localhost`:

```bash
export NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://{{projectName}}-web.<subdomain>.workers.dev
```

**2. Write the secrets of the first deploy** to `apps/web/.prod.env` (gitignored; a new Worker does not exist yet, so `wrangler secret put` cannot run). `BETTER_AUTH_URL` is the same URL:

```bash
printf 'BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=%s\nBETTER_AUTH_URL=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)" "$NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL" > apps/web/.prod.env
```

**3. Build the web Worker:** `{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{runScript "build:cf"}}{{/inDirectory}}` (wrangler types → `opennextjs-cloudflare build`, which runs `next build` once through the `build` script).

**4. Deploy it:** `{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{runScript "deploy"}} -- --secrets-file .prod.env{{/inDirectory}}` (`opennextjs-cloudflare deploy`). Later deploys are `{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{runScript "deploy"}}{{/inDirectory}}`, with the secrets already in place.

**5. Delete the secrets file.** The Worker holds the secrets now, and `.prod.env` keeps them in clear text on disk:

```bash
rm apps/web/.prod.env
```

**6. Deploy the cron Worker:** `{{#inDirectory "apps" "cron"}}{{runScript "deploy"}}{{/inDirectory}}` (`wrangler deploy`). The `triggers.crons` schedule (`0 3 * * *` — daily at 03:00 UTC) is registered on deploy.

**Build where `apps/web/.env` holds no real secret.** `build:cf` bundles every `.env*` value, server secrets included, into the Worker (`.open-next/cloudflare/next-env.mjs`) as a `process.env` fallback, with no opt-out. A secret set with `wrangler secret put` or `--secrets-file` wins at runtime, but the bundled value is used silently when a secret was never set. `.env` is for local development: build the Worker you deploy in CI, or from a checkout that has none.

