## Local development
- `cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env` and set `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` to a value generated for local use only (the README first-run step gives the command): `build:cf` bundles it into the Worker (see Build & deploy). `BETTER_AUTH_URL` is already `{{appUrl "web"}}`. Next.js reads `.env`; in dev mode Better Auth trusts `localhost`.
- `.env.seed` at the repo root holds `SEED_PASSWORD`, the password of the seeded accounts (`password` by default); `{{runScript "local-setup"}}` creates it from `.env.seed.example`. It is a file of its own, loaded only by the root `db:seed`, because `build:cf` bundles every `.env*` it finds in `apps/web` and at the repo root (`.env`, `.env.local`, their mode variants) into the Worker, and a seed password has no place in a deployed bundle.
- `{{runScript "local-setup"}}` once (applies local migrations + seeds the local D1).
- `{{runScript "dev"}}` — `next dev` with the Cloudflare bindings wired via `initOpenNextCloudflareForDev` (D1/R2 from `.wrangler`, the same local state `local-setup` seeds). The cron Worker runs next to it under `wrangler dev` on port {{appPort "cron"}} (`apps/cron`, same local state). This is the primary local loop (hot reload, dev-mode auth).
- `{{#inDirectory "apps" "web"}}{{runScript "preview"}}{{/inDirectory}}` runs the OpenNext **production** build locally to smoke-test the deployed shape. The build freezes the values of `.env` into the Worker, so run `build:cf` again after every `.env` change. Wrangler does not load `.env` for this preview (OpenNext handles it), so it warns about a missing required secret even when `.env` holds it; the warning is safe to ignore then.
{{> cloudflare/preview-origin}}
- Fire the cron handler locally without waiting for the schedule, while `{{runScript "dev"}}` runs: `curl -X POST "http://localhost:{{appPort "cron"}}/cdn-cgi/local/explorer/api/local/scheduled?worker={{#with projectName}}{{this}}{{/with}}-cron" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"cron":"0 3 * * *"}'` runs `scheduled()` against the local D1 + R2 the web app uses (purges expired documents).

## Build-time vs runtime env
Bindings (`DB`, `STORAGE`) are **runtime**, read through `getEnv()` (web) or the `Env` arg (cron), never at module load. Secrets are runtime too: set them with `wrangler secret put`, never rely on `.env`, whose values are bundled into the Worker at build. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` and other build-time config come from the environment of the build: `.env` for a local build, the exported `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` for the one you deploy. Keep the D1/R2 access request- or event-scoped; see [Data layer](data-layer.md) and [Storage](storage.md).
